{"reference":"1 Kings 8","book":"1 Kings","chapter":8,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Reading the Temple Prayer","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"Some interpreters compare Solomon’s temple with other Levantine and ancient Near Eastern temples because of shared architectural and craft features. That comparison does not, however, account for the chapter’s own emphasis: Solomon’s prayer centers God’s name, covenant, forgiveness, prayer, and the inclusion of foreigners, while verse 27 insists that even heaven cannot contain God, much less the house Solomon built.","verse_refs":["1 Kings 8:22-53"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_1_kings_008_9229bf64f555260901d7c852"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"},{"id":"block_2","text":"The chapter uses sanctuary language of dwelling and holiness, but similar language elsewhere should not erase the distinctions among the tabernacle, the First and Second Temples, later Christian metaphors, a heavenly sanctuary, and new creation. In particular, later Christian readings of sanctuary imagery cannot rightly support antisemitism, replacement claims, the destruction or seizure of holy places, or immunity from ethical accountability.","verse_refs":["1 Kings 8:10-13","1 Kings 8:27-53"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_1_kings_008_9880a98fc05e83d2509c136e"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}